---
// ============================================================
// FILE: src/pages/build/index.astro
// Phase 4 — Guided build, Step 1 of 10 (replaces Phase 1 stub).
// Long-form reading-width page, same convention as Get Ready:
// max-w-3xl column, plain headed sections, Callout rendered
// directly — no wrapping card.
// ============================================================
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Guided build — PC Customization Manual">
  <article class="mx-auto w-full max-w-content px-6 py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-medium md:text-3xl">Guided build</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-secondary">
        Follow these steps in order — motherboard prep happens outside the case
        first, then everything comes together. Skipping ahead is how cables end
        up trapped under components that are already screwed down.
      </p>

      <!-- Step 1 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-1">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 1 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Open the case</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          Before installing anything, get familiar with your case — where
          things go, and what you'll need later.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            Place the case on a stable, well-lit surface with the side panel
            facing up.
          </li>
          <li>
            Remove the side panel(s) — usually secured with thumbscrews or a
            latch. Check your case's manual if yours is different.
          </li>
          <li>
            Locate the motherboard standoffs already installed inside the case.
            Confirm their positions match your motherboard's screw holes for its
            form factor.
          </li>
          <li>
            Locate the front-panel connector headers near the bottom-front of the
            case — power switch, reset switch, power LED, HDD LED, front-panel
            USB and audio. Note their labels now; you'll connect these in step 9.
          </li>
          <li>
            If your case has cable routing channels or grommets, note their
            locations — you'll route cables through these for a clean build.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="caution" title="Standoff placement">
            If your case's pre-installed standoffs don't match your motherboard's
            form factor (e.g., positioned for Micro-ATX when your board is ATX),
            reposition them before mounting the board. A standoff under the wrong
            spot can short the board against the case.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/case/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the Case component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 2 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-2">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 2 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Install the CPU</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With the motherboard still outside the case, install the CPU now —
          it's far easier to see and align the socket before anything else is
          in the way.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>Open the CPU socket's retention lever/arm on the motherboard.</li>
          <li>
            Locate the alignment marker on the CPU (a small triangle or notch,
            depending on socket type) and the matching marker on the socket.
          </li>
          <li>
            Align the CPU using that marker and lower it straight down into the
            socket. Never force it — it should drop in with no pressure.
          </li>
          <li>Close the retention lever/arm to lock the CPU in place.</li>
          <li>
            If your socket has a protective cover attached to the lever, leave it
            on — it will pop off on its own when you install the cooler in the
            next step.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="danger" title="Never touch the pins">
            Whether the pins are on the CPU (AMD) or in the socket (Intel), never
            touch them directly. A single bent pin can make the CPU or motherboard
            unusable, and it's typically not covered by warranty.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/cpu/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the CPU component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 3 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-3">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 3 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Install the CPU cooler</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With the CPU installed, mount the cooler now — it's much easier to
          access the socket area before the board goes into the case.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            If your cooler doesn't come with thermal paste pre-applied to its
            base, apply a small pea-sized dot in the center of the CPU's heat
            spreader.
          </li>
          <li>
            Remove the CPU socket's protective cover if it's still attached — the
            cooler won't seat correctly with it in place.
          </li>
          <li>
            Position the cooler's mounting bracket over the CPU, aligning it with
            the retention holes or bracket around the socket.
          </li>
          <li>
            Tighten the mounting screws gradually, alternating between opposite
            corners in a star pattern rather than fully tightening one screw at a
            time — this seats the cooler evenly and avoids uneven pressure on the
            CPU.
          </li>
          <li>
            Connect the cooler's fan (and pump, if liquid-cooled) cable to the
            motherboard's CPU_FAN header, typically located near the socket.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="caution" title="Even pressure matters">
            Tightening one screw fully before moving to the next can seat the cooler
            at an angle, causing uneven contact with the CPU and reduced cooling.
            Always alternate between opposite corners in small increments.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/cpu-cooler/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the CPU Cooler component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 4 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-4">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 4 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Install RAM</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With the CPU and cooler in place, install RAM next — the slots are
          easy to access before the board is mounted in the case.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            Check your motherboard's manual for which slots to use first if
            you're not filling all of them — common with 2 of 4 slots for
            dual-channel.
          </li>
          <li>Open the retention clips at both ends of the RAM slot you're using.</li>
          <li>
            Align the notch on the RAM stick with the key in the slot — it only
            fits one way.
          </li>
          <li>
            Press down firmly and evenly on both ends until the retention clips
            snap closed on their own.
          </li>
          <li>Repeat for each additional stick, following the same slot pattern.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="caution" title="Even pressure, both ends">
            Pushing down on only one end of the stick can seat it at an angle,
            causing boot failures or the board to fail to detect the RAM. Press both
            ends evenly until you hear both clips click.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/ram/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the RAM component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 5 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-5">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 5 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Mount the motherboard in the case</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With the CPU, cooler, and RAM installed, the motherboard is ready to
          go into the case.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            Install the rear I/O shield into the matching cutout on the back of the
            case, if it's not pre-installed — orient it so it matches your
            motherboard's port layout, and press until it clicks into place.
          </li>
          <li>
            Lower the motherboard into the case at an angle, aligning its rear ports
            with the I/O shield's openings.
          </li>
          <li>
            Set the board down onto the standoffs and check that every screw hole
            lines up with a standoff underneath — don't force a screw into a hole
            that isn't backed by a standoff.
          </li>
          <li>
            Insert and hand-tighten each mounting screw, working in a diagonal
            pattern across the board rather than one side at a time.
          </li>
          <li>
            Once all screws are started, snug them down evenly — firm, but don't
            overtighten. The board should feel solid with no flex.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="danger" title="Check every standoff">
            A screw hole without a standoff beneath it can let the board flex and short
            against the case when tightened. If you see a mounting hole with no
            standoff underneath, either add one matching your board's form factor, or
            skip that screw.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/motherboard/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the Motherboard component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 6 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 6 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Install the power supply</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With the motherboard mounted, install the power supply next — it's
          easier to route its cables now, before the case fills up with other
          components.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            Determine your case's PSU mounting orientation — most modern cases
            mount the PSU at the bottom, with its fan facing either down through
            a dust filter or up into the case, depending on airflow design.
          </li>
          <li>
            Slide the PSU into its mounting bay and secure it with the provided
            screws through the back of the case.
          </li>
          <li>
            If your PSU is modular, connect only the cables you need — before or
            after mounting, whichever is easier to route in your case.
          </li>
          <li>
            Route the main 24-pin motherboard power cable and the CPU power cable
            (4/8-pin) toward the motherboard, but don't connect them yet — you'll
            do that during cable management.
          </li>
          <li>
            Leave unused modular cables disconnected and set aside; extra cables
            you don't need only make airflow and cable management harder.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="caution" title="Check the voltage switch">
            If your PSU has a manual voltage selector switch (110V/220V), confirm it
            matches your region's power supply before plugging in. The wrong setting
            can damage the PSU or other components when powered on.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/psu/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the PSU component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 7 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-7">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 7 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Install the GPU</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With the PSU in place, install the GPU next — you'll need its power
          cables ready, which you already routed in the last step.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            Locate the primary PCIe x16 slot on the motherboard — usually the
            one closest to the CPU — and check your case for the matching
            expansion slot covers to remove on the back.
          </li>
          <li>
            Remove the expansion slot covers on the back of the case that align
            with the GPU's width (most modern GPUs span two or three slots).
          </li>
          <li>
            Open the PCIe slot's retention latch, then align the GPU's connector
            with the slot and press straight down until it clicks into place.
          </li>
          <li>
            Secure the GPU's mounting bracket to the case with screws through the
            removed slot covers' openings.
          </li>
          <li>
            Connect the required PCIe power cables from the PSU to the GPU —
            check your GPU's documentation for how many it needs.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="caution" title="Support the weight">
            Larger GPUs can sag under their own weight over time, straining the
            PCIe slot. If your card is heavy, consider a support bracket —
            especially if the case will be moved after the build.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/gpu/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the GPU component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 8 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 8 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Install storage</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With the GPU installed, add storage next — the last major component
          before cable management.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            For M.2 NVMe drives, locate the M.2 slot(s) on the motherboard —
            often under a heatsink or shroud — and remove any retaining screw or
            clip before inserting the drive at an angle.
          </li>
          <li>
            Insert the M.2 drive into its slot at roughly a 30-degree angle, then
            press it down flat and secure it with the retaining screw.
          </li>
          <li>
            For 2.5" or 3.5" SATA drives, mount the drive into a drive bay or
            tray using the provided screws or tool-less mounting clips.
          </li>
          <li>
            Connect a SATA data cable from the drive to an available SATA port on
            the motherboard.
          </li>
          <li>
            Connect a SATA power cable from the PSU to the drive — most modular
            PSU cables include several SATA power connectors on one cable run.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="note" title="M.2 heatsinks">
            Some motherboards ship with a small heatsink covering the M.2 slot.
            Remove it before installing the drive, and reattach it afterward —
            running an NVMe drive without its heatsink can cause thermal
            throttling under sustained load.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/storage/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the Storage component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 9 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-9">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 9 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">Cable management & front-panel headers</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          With every component installed, it's time to connect the front-panel
          headers and tidy the cables — this is what separates a build that just
          works from one that's actually pleasant to live with.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            Connect the front-panel headers you identified in step 1 — power
            switch, reset switch, power LED, and HDD LED — to their labeled pins on
            the motherboard, usually located along the bottom-front edge. Check
            your motherboard's manual for the exact pinout; these headers are small
            and easy to misalign.
          </li>
          <li>
            Connect the front-panel USB and audio headers the same way, matching
            each connector's label to its corresponding motherboard header.
          </li>
          <li>
            Connect the 24-pin motherboard power cable and the CPU power cable
            (4/8-pin) that you routed in step 6.
          </li>
          <li>
            Route remaining cables along the back of the motherboard tray or
            through your case's cable channels, keeping them away from fans and out
            of the GPU's airflow path.
          </li>
          <li>
            Bundle excess cable length with ties or velcro straps, and tuck it
            behind the motherboard tray or in unused drive bays.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="caution" title="Front-panel header polarity">
            Power and reset switch headers usually don't have polarity, but LED
            headers do — connecting one backwards just means the LED won't light, not
            a hazard, but it's worth checking your motherboard's manual if an LED
            doesn't turn on after first boot.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/components/case/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See the Case component page →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Step 10 of 10 -->
      <section class="mt-12" id="step-10">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary">Step 10 of 10</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-xl font-medium">First boot & BIOS</h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary">
          This is it — time to power on for the first time and confirm everything
          is working before you close up the case.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-base text-primary">
          <li>
            Connect your monitor, keyboard, and mouse, then plug the power supply
            into the wall and switch it on at the back of the PSU.
          </li>
          <li>
            Press the power button. Fans should spin, and you should see your
            motherboard's logo or POST screen within a few seconds — don't close
            the case yet, in case something needs adjusting.
          </li>
          <li>
            If nothing happens, don't panic — double-check the 24-pin and CPU power
            connections first; a loose power cable is the most common cause of a
            no-boot on a fresh build.
          </li>
          <li>
            Once you see the POST screen, enter the BIOS — usually by pressing
            Delete or F2 repeatedly during boot — and confirm your CPU, RAM
            capacity, and storage drive are all correctly detected.
          </li>
          <li>
            If everything is detected correctly, exit the BIOS, install your
            operating system, and only then close up the case and route any final
            cables.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <Callout variant="danger" title="No display, no POST">
            If you get no display and no beeps or POST after checking power
            connections, power off immediately and reseat the RAM and GPU one at a
            time before trying again. Continuing to power cycle a system that won't
            POST can indicate a deeper issue worth diagnosing before further attempts.
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/troubleshooting/"
            class="rounded-card text-sm font-medium text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            See Troubleshooting & reference →
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
